db/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565355 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565355 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186564957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565355 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "617bb124-5500-5301-bf14-9faf85d05ab0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565363 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565129 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565363 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565363 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565363 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565363 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565363 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565363 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565363 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186565273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565363 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "851cb76b-3dab-5e83-b29a-5e7d934c5feb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565391 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565391 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565037 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565391 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565052 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565391 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565165 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565391 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565391 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565391 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565391 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186565240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565391 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "da5b101c-53ff-558b-b46b-69d15ea46f42" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565416 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186565391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565416 #edb/ref 1069 "q-128449" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565416 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565416 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $P(0,1)$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{3x+2}{1-3y}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565416 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "240c650f-50a8-531d-9d84-394b65ab5cda" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565416 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the value of the function at $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,1) & =\\frac{3(0)+2}{1-3(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+2}{1-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $P(0,1)$ is the $-1$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=-1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{3x+2}{1-3y} & =-1 \\\\ 3x+2 & =-(1-3y) \\\\ 3x+2 & =3y-1 \\\\ 3y & =3x+3 \\\\ y & =x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $P(0,1)$ is the line $y=x+1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565428 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186565363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565428 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71206" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565428 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565428 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $H(\\sqrt{3},5)$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{6}{y-{x}^{2}}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565428 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4f8639b5-fe7e-594e-a639-5523842bd2ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565428 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the value of the function at $H$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\sqrt{3},5) & =\\frac{6}{5-(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $H(\\sqrt{3},5)$ is the $3$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=3.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{6}{y-{x}^{2}} & =3 \\\\ 2 & =y-{x}^{2} \\\\ y & ={x}^{2}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $H(\\sqrt{3},5)$ is the parabola $y={x}^{2}+2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565429 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186565355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565429 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71219" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565429 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565429 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $F(\\sqrt{7},1)$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{12y}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565429 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "54914efc-dfc4-51a5-a306-07132cb10e69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565429 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the value of the function at $F$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\sqrt{7},1) & =\\frac{12(1)}{(\\sqrt{7})^{2}-{1}^{2}}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $F(\\sqrt{7},1)$ is the $2$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=2.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{12y}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & =2 \\\\ 6y & ={x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ -{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+6y & =0 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+6y+9 & =9 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $F(\\sqrt{7},1)$ is the hyperbola $-{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=9.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565465 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186565340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565465 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129057" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565465 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565465 #edb/ref 1071 "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1}{{y}^{2}+1}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $c=0$ gives an ellipse\n\nII. $c=1$ gives a circle\n\nIII. $c=2$ gives a hyperbola" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565465 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cdadd58a-68d8-50fc-902f-9f8e63435f08" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565465 #edb/ref 1074 "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1}{{y}^{2}+1} & =c \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1 & =c({y}^{2}+1) \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+(1-c){y}^{2} & =c+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=0,$ the level curve is $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ which is an ellipse. So, I is true.\n\n- If $c=1,$ the level curves are $x=\\pm 1,$ which consists of two lines. So, II is false.\n\n- If $c=2,$ the level curve is $2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=3,$ which is a hyperbola. So, III is true.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
